 This problem is asking us to determine how many integers n from 1 to 100, inclusive, can be transformed into 1 by repeatedly applying the function \( f \). The function \( f(n) \) is defined as: \[
f(n) = \begin{cases} 
n^2 + 1 & \text{if } n \text{ is odd} \\ 
\frac{n}{2} & \text{if } n \text{ is even} 
\end{cases}
\]
We need to check how many starting values from 1 to 100, inclusive, will eventually reach 1 through repeated applications of this function. We can simulate the function for each number from 1 to 100 and check if it eventually reaches 1. However, for this problem, we can observe that if \( n \) is even, it will be divided by 2 until it becomes odd. And if \( n \) is odd, \( n^2 + 1 \) will be even, and then it will be divided by 2 until it becomes odd again. So, we only need to check the odd numbers because eventually all starting numbers will reduce to an odd number and eventually get to 1. For example, if we start with an odd number \( n \), the next number will be \( n^2 + 1 \), which is even, and then it will be divided by 2. So we can reduce this problem to checking only the odd numbers. The odd numbers from 1 to 100 are 1, 3, 5, ..., 99. There are 50 such numbers. <answer>50</answer>

